# Skia Canvas Backend for ESP32-S3 [SPEC:SKIA-ESP32S3]

> ESP32-S3 with PSRAM runs Skia's CPU rasterizer directly, producing pixel-identical output to the desktop Skia backend. One rendering engine, two classes of embedded target: ESP32 (custom renderer, resource-constrained) and ESP32-S3 (Skia, pixel-identical to desktop).

---

## 1. Rationale [SKIA-S3-RATIONALE]

The custom `canvas_esp32.cpp` backend was designed for the ESP32-D0WD (CYD) — 520KB SRAM, no PSRAM, 4MB flash. It uses integer arithmetic, SDF-based AA, and band rendering to fit within these constraints. The tradeoffs produce visible pixel-level divergence from the desktop Skia backend: staircase corners, different alpha blending, coarser anti-aliasing.

ESP32-S3 boards eliminate these constraints:

| Resource | ESP32 (CYD) | ESP32-S3 | ESP32-P4 |
|----------|-------------|----------|----------|
| SRAM | 520KB | 512KB | 768KB |
| PSRAM | None | 2-8MB (QSPI/OPI) | 2-32MB (OPI) |
| Flash | 4MB | 4-16MB | 4-16MB |
| CPU | Dual 240MHz | Dual 240MHz + vector instructions | Single 400MHz RISC-V + LP core |
| Display interface | SPI only | SPI, I2C, parallel 8/16-bit, LCD-CAM | MIPI-DSI, parallel RGB888, LCD-CAM |

With 2-8MB PSRAM, a full 240x320 RGB8888 framebuffer (307KB) or even 480x320 (614KB) fits comfortably. Skia's CPU rasterizer binary (~1-2MB) fits in flash. The Skia approach becomes viable and eliminates all geometric rendering divergence.

For larger displays (800x480 and above), the ESP32-P4 is the stronger target. Its MIPI-DSI interface drives high-resolution IPS/OLED panels directly without SPI bandwidth limits. 32MB PSRAM accommodates a full 800x480 RGBA8888 framebuffer (1.5MB) with room for Skia internals and double-buffering. The 400MHz RISC-V core provides ~1.7x the single-thread throughput of the ESP32-S3, reducing frame render times for complex widget compositions. The same `SkiaEmbeddedCanvas` backend targets both — only the display driver and PlatformIO board config differ.

---

## 2. Architecture [SKIA-S3-ARCH]

### 2.1 Backend Selection [SKIA-S3-ARCH-SELECT]

Two C++ Canvas backends coexist in the firmware source:

| Backend | Target | File | Selection |
|---------|--------|------|-----------|
| `ESP32Canvas` | ESP32 (no PSRAM) | `canvas_esp32.cpp` | `#if !CONFIG_SPIRAM` or build flag |
| `SkiaEmbeddedCanvas` | ESP32-S3 (PSRAM) | `canvas_skia_embedded.cpp` | `#if CONFIG_SPIRAM` or build flag |

Both implement the same `Canvas` abstract interface from `canvas.h`. Widget code, main loop, touch dispatch — all unchanged. Only the rendering backend differs.

### 2.2 Rendering Pipeline [SKIA-S3-ARCH-PIPELINE]

```
Widget.draw(canvas, rect)
  → Canvas drawing commands (fill_rect, fill_arc, draw_text, ...)
  → SkiaEmbeddedCanvas translates to Skia API calls
  → Skia CPU rasterizer renders to SkBitmap (RGB8888 in PSRAM)
  → present() converts RGB8888 → RGB565 and DMA transfers to display
```

### 2.3 Memory Layout [SKIA-S3-ARCH-MEMORY]

| Buffer | Size (240x320) | Size (480x320) | Location |
|--------|---------------|-----------------|----------|
| Skia SkBitmap (RGBA8888) | 307KB | 614KB | PSRAM |
| Display transfer buffer (RGB565) | 150KB or band | 300KB or band | PSRAM or SRAM |
| Skia internal allocator | ~200-500KB | ~200-500KB | PSRAM |
| Widget tree + app logic | ~10-50KB | ~10-50KB | SRAM |

Total PSRAM for 240x320: ~700KB-1MB. Well within 2MB minimum.

For larger displays (480x320 or 320x480), band rendering can be used on the Skia side too — render to a Skia surface sized to the band, convert, DMA, advance. This caps PSRAM usage at band_height * width * 4 bytes per band.

---

## 3. Skia Integration [SKIA-S3-INTEGRATION]

### 3.1 Skia Build Configuration [SKIA-S3-INTEGRATION-BUILD]

Skia supports embedded targets via its GN build system. Required configuration:

| Flag | Value | Rationale |
|------|-------|-----------|
| `skia_use_gl` | false | No GPU — CPU rasterizer only |
| `skia_use_vulkan` | false | No GPU |
| `skia_enable_gpu` | false | Saves ~500KB |
| `skia_use_freetype` | true | Font rendering — use bitmap atlas instead if too large |
| `skia_use_harfbuzz` | false | No complex text shaping needed |
| `skia_use_icu` | false | No internationalization |
| `skia_use_libjpeg_turbo_decode` | false | PNG only sufficient |
| `skia_use_libpng_decode` | true | PNG asset loading |
| `skia_use_zlib` | true | Required by libpng |
| `target_cpu` | "arm" | ESP32-S3 is Xtensa LX7, needs custom toolchain config |

Target binary size estimate: 1-2MB (CPU rasterizer + basic font + PNG decode). Fits in 4MB flash with room for app code and font atlas.

**Toolchain note**: ESP32-S3 uses Xtensa LX7 (not ARM). Skia's build system needs a custom toolchain definition pointing to the ESP-IDF Xtensa compiler. This is the primary integration challenge — Skia has been built for ARM embedded but not Xtensa. Alternative: use Skia's portable C++ path (no SIMD), which compiles on any C++17 toolchain.

### 3.2 SkiaEmbeddedCanvas Implementation [SKIA-S3-INTEGRATION-CANVAS]

The `SkiaEmbeddedCanvas` class wraps Skia's C++ API:

```cpp
class SkiaEmbeddedCanvas : public Canvas {
public:
    void create_surface(int w, int h) override {
        // Allocate SkBitmap in PSRAM
        _bitmap.allocPixels(SkImageInfo::MakeN32Premul(w, h));
        _canvas = std::make_unique<SkCanvas>(_bitmap);
    }

    void fill_rect(float x, float y, float w, float h) override {
        _canvas->drawRect(SkRect::MakeXYWH(x, y, w, h), _fill_paint);
    }

    void fill_circle(float cx, float cy, float r) override {
        _canvas->drawCircle(cx, cy, r, _fill_paint);
    }

    void fill_round_rect(float x, float y, float w, float h, float radius) override {
        _canvas->drawRoundRect(SkRect::MakeXYWH(x, y, w, h), radius, radius, _fill_paint);
    }

    void draw_text(const char* text, float x, float y, const char* align) override {
        // Use bitmap font atlas (same as ESP32Canvas) for cross-platform text fidelity
        // OR use Skia native text for maximum quality on high-DPI displays
    }

    void present() override {
        // Convert RGBA8888 → RGB565, DMA to display
        _display->transfer_framebuffer(_rgb565_buf, 0, _height - 1);
    }

private:
    SkBitmap _bitmap;                    // PSRAM-allocated
    std::unique_ptr<SkCanvas> _canvas;
    SkPaint _fill_paint, _stroke_paint;
    IDisplay* _display;                  // Abstract display interface
};
```

The API mapping is near-1:1 — each Canvas method is a single Skia call. The implementation should be ~200-300 lines.

### 3.3 Font Rendering Strategy [SKIA-S3-INTEGRATION-FONT]

Two options, selectable at build time:

| Option | Approach | Fidelity target |
|--------|----------|----------------|
| **Atlas mode** | Same bitmap font atlas as ESP32Canvas and desktop atlas mode | Pixel-identical to CYD and desktop-with-atlas |
| **Skia native** | Skia's built-in FreeType text rendering | Pixel-identical to desktop-native, highest quality |

For cross-platform visual QA (pixel comparison against CYD), use atlas mode. For production displays where quality matters more than CYD matching, use Skia native.

---

## 4. Display Abstraction [SKIA-S3-DISPLAY]

### 4.1 Display Interface [SKIA-S3-DISPLAY-IFACE]

Abstract the display hardware behind an interface so the Canvas backend works with any display controller:

```cpp
class IDisplay {
public:
    virtual ~IDisplay() = default;
    virtual void init() = 0;
    virtual void transfer_framebuffer(const uint16_t* buf, uint16_t y_start, uint16_t y_end) = 0;
    virtual void transfer_band(const uint16_t* buf, uint16_t y_start, uint16_t y_end) = 0;
    virtual uint16_t width() const = 0;
    virtual uint16_t height() const = 0;
};
```

Existing `ILI9341` class implements this for the CYD. New display drivers implement the same interface:

| Display | Controller | Interface | Resolution | Notes |
|---------|-----------|-----------|------------|-------|
| CYD TFT | ILI9341 | SPI | 240x320 | Existing driver |
| OLED | SSD1351 / SH1107 | SPI / I2C | 128x128 to 256x64 | High contrast, true black |
| IPS TFT | ST7789 / ILI9488 | SPI / parallel | 240x320 to 480x320 | Wide viewing angle |
| Round OLED | GC9A01 | SPI | 240x240 | Circular gauge displays |

### 4.2 RGB8888 to RGB565 Conversion [SKIA-S3-DISPLAY-CONVERT]

Skia renders in RGBA8888. Displays accept RGB565. Conversion at `present()` time:

```cpp
inline uint16_t rgba8888_to_rgb565(uint32_t pixel) {
    uint8_t r = (pixel >> 16) & 0xFF;
    uint8_t g = (pixel >> 8) & 0xFF;
    uint8_t b = pixel & 0xFF;
    return ((r >> 3) << 11) | ((g >> 2) << 5) | (b >> 3);
}
```

This conversion is the only source of color quantization. On displays that accept RGB888 (some OLED controllers), skip the conversion entirely — pass Skia's buffer directly.

---

## 5. Build System [SKIA-S3-BUILD]

### 5.1 PlatformIO Configuration [SKIA-S3-BUILD-PIO]

New PlatformIO environment alongside the existing CYD environment:

```ini
[env:esp32s3_skia]
platform = espressif32
board = esp32-s3-devkitc-1
framework = espidf
board_build.cmake_extra_args =
    -DUSE_SKIA_BACKEND=ON
    -DSKIA_DIR=${PROJECT_DIR}/lib/skia
```

### 5.2 Skia as ESP-IDF Component [SKIA-S3-BUILD-COMPONENT]

Pre-build Skia as a static library for Xtensa LX7 and include as an ESP-IDF component:

```
cyd/
├── components/
│   └── skia/
│       ├── CMakeLists.txt      # ESP-IDF component wrapper
│       ├── include/             # Skia headers (subset)
│       └── lib/
│           └── libskia.a       # Pre-built for xtensa-esp32s3-elf
```

Pre-building avoids compiling Skia from source during firmware builds (~30 min). The pre-built library is committed or fetched by a build script.

### 5.3 Conditional Compilation [SKIA-S3-BUILD-CONDITIONAL]

```cpp
// canvas_factory.h
#if defined(USE_SKIA_BACKEND) && CONFIG_SPIRAM
    #include "canvas_skia_embedded.h"
    using PlatformCanvas = SkiaEmbeddedCanvas;
#else
    #include "canvas_esp32.h"
    using PlatformCanvas = ESP32Canvas;
#endif
```

`main.cpp` uses `PlatformCanvas` — same code, different backend selected at build time.

---

## 6. Pixel Comparison Validation [SKIA-S3-VALIDATION]

### 6.1 Expected Convergence [SKIA-S3-VALIDATION-TARGET]

| Comparison | Before (custom renderer) | After (Skia on S3) |
|-----------|-------------------------|-------------------|
| Desktop-atlas vs ESP32 | 55% near-match, max delta 136 | **>99% near-match** (only RGB565 quantization) |
| Geometric AA divergence | Visible on all curves | **Eliminated** — same Skia rasterizer |
| Text divergence (with atlas) | Near-zero | Near-zero (unchanged) |

The only remaining divergence should be RGB565 color quantization (±3 per channel), which the pixel comparison tool already accounts for.

### 6.2 Automated QA [SKIA-S3-VALIDATION-QA]

Same pipeline as CYD:
1. ESP32-S3 renders dashboard with Skia → captures BMP → pushes to document_store
2. Desktop renders same dashboard with Skia → PNG
3. `pixel_comparison.py` compares → expect >99% near-match
4. Screenshots saved to `cyd/screenshots/progression/` with device tag

---

## 7. Phase Plan [SKIA-S3-PHASES]

| Phase | Scope | Deliverable |
|-------|-------|-------------|
| S3-1 | Skia cross-compilation | `libskia.a` built for xtensa-esp32s3-elf, basic drawing test |
| S3-2 | SkiaEmbeddedCanvas implementation | All Canvas methods mapped to Skia, rendering to PSRAM bitmap |
| S3-3 | Display driver abstraction | `IDisplay` interface, ST7789/SSD1351 drivers alongside ILI9341 |
| S3-4 | present() pipeline | RGBA8888→RGB565 conversion, DMA transfer, band rendering option |
| S3-5 | Font atlas integration | Bitmap atlas text rendering via Skia surface, matching CYD output |
| S3-6 | Pixel comparison validation | Automated QA showing >99% near-match vs desktop |

### 7.1 Risk: Xtensa Toolchain [SKIA-S3-RISK-XTENSA]

Skia's build system (GN) does not natively support Xtensa. Approaches:

1. **CMake wrapper**: Build Skia source files directly via ESP-IDF's CMake, bypassing GN. Requires enumerating source files manually but avoids toolchain configuration. Community projects (e.g., esp-idf-skia) may exist.
2. **GN cross-compile**: Add Xtensa toolchain definition to Skia's GN configs. More complex but maintainable.
3. **Portable C++ only**: Disable all SIMD/platform-specific code paths. Slower but guaranteed to compile on any C++17 toolchain including Xtensa.

Recommendation: start with approach 3 (portable C++) to validate feasibility, then optimize with approach 1 for build integration.

---

## 8. Compliance [SKIA-S3-COMPLIANCE]

| ID | Requirement | Verification |
|----|-------------|-------------|
| SS-C1 | SkiaEmbeddedCanvas implements all Canvas interface methods | Compiles without pure virtual errors, renders test dashboard |
| SS-C2 | Same dashboard renders identically on desktop Skia and ESP32-S3 Skia | pixel_comparison.py: >99% near-match (RGB565 tolerance) |
| SS-C3 | PSRAM allocation succeeds for framebuffer + Skia internals | Boot log shows successful allocation, heap report |
| SS-C4 | Frame rendering completes within acceptable time | <500ms for full 240x320 frame (target: <200ms) |
| SS-C5 | Firmware builds with Skia included | Flash usage <80% on 4MB flash |
| SS-C6 | canvas_esp32.cpp unchanged and still builds for ESP32 targets | `pio run -e cyd` succeeds with no modifications |
| SS-C7 | Display abstraction supports ILI9341 and at least one new controller | Two display drivers implement IDisplay, both render correctly |
| SS-C8 | Band rendering option works on Skia backend | For displays >240x320, PSRAM-efficient band rendering produces correct output |

